WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:07.339 align:start size:97% position:3%
STEVE: Just opened up my book,
started reading the first
sentence in the paragraph when

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.510 align:start size:88% position:13%
I heard what sounded like
a freight train coming
through my bedroom.

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:16.349
I jumped out of the
bed and then there
was a thumping sound

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:19.051
that consecutively
got much faster.

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:22.856 align:start size:91% position:9%
And with each thump I felt
the rig actually shake.

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:32.399
Then there was
an initial boom.

00:00:33.132 --> 00:00:34.700
The lights went out.

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:36.436 align:start size:81% position:19%
MIKE: And there
was a huge explosion.

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:45.178
<i> REPORTER (over TV): The
 explosion rocked the rig
 about 10:00 Central Time,</i>

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:47.047 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> spraying flames
 in every direction.</i>

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.586
STEVE: Well, I saw fire from
derrick leg to derrick leg.

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:56.422 align:start size:84% position:16%
PROFESSOR: When you
have oil and gas under
pressure, underground,

00:00:56.522 --> 00:00:57.957
it erupts upwards.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:01.361 align:start size:78% position:22%
RIG WORKER: It
was extremely loud.

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:04.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
It actually started
sounding like a living thing.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:11.570 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> REPORTER (over TV):
 It's the sound of escaping
 gas feeding the fireball</i>

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.873
<i> incinerating the
 Deepwater Horizon.</i>

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:21.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
RIG WORKER: It's like
you're almost waiting to die.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.850
There's people screaming.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.852
You feel the heat
from the fire.

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:30.590 align:start size:81% position:19%
Scared to death,
I figured this is my
time, I'm gonna die.

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:35.328
<i> REPORTER (over TV): 126
 people were on board at
 the time of the explosion.</i>

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:39.032 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> REPORTER (over TV):
 Some jumped more than
 75 feet into the ocean.</i>

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:45.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
CURRY: The Coast Guard is
pressing its search today
for 11 workers still missing.

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:48.941
JAMES: I started to have a bad
feeling when the sun came up.

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:51.978 align:start size:88% position:13%
It got very
precarious at that time.

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:55.181
Time was running out.

00:01:56.482 --> 00:02:02.822
(theme music plays).

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:16.369 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:32.985 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
FRANK: Deepwater Horizon,
a state of the art,
semi-submersible,

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:36.055 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
dynamically positioned
by satellite drilling rig.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:44.563 line:0%
And Transocean rents
Deepwater Horizon to
the big oil companies

00:02:44.663 --> 00:02:46.532 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
at about a half-million
dollars a day.

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:51.270 align:start size:91% position:9%
Now, what the oil companies
use Deepwater Horizon for
is to drill the wells.

00:02:52.371 --> 00:02:57.509 align:start position:0%
They come out, they drill in
really challenging environments,
they find the oil,

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:01.881 align:start size:88% position:13%
sometimes they set up
the production well, then
they go to the next job.

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:06.419 align:start size:84% position:16%
CHRIS: It is different
than any other job.

00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:13.426
A lot of people when they
first start out they try to
compare it to construction,

00:03:14.626 --> 00:03:16.528 align:start size:81% position:19%
and it's a completely
different thing.

00:03:17.162 --> 00:03:18.765
Being away from your family.

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:21.768
You know, something happens
you can't just leave, it,
it's all weather permitting.

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:28.508 align:start size:91% position:9%
RIG WORKER: These are
our charts, and this is our
tracking of hurricane Ivan.

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:32.078
Put a mark on that piece
of paper where we's at.

00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:38.484 align:start size:88% position:13%
CHRIS: Everybody out
there gets kind of close,

00:03:38.584 --> 00:03:40.486
I mean as far as
friendships and
stuff like that.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:41.888 align:start size:84% position:16%
RIG WORKER: Are you
gonna do some work now?

00:03:43.555 --> 00:03:46.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
CHRIS: Really I'm with
them more than I am my
own family most of the time.

00:03:47.993 --> 00:03:49.262 align:start size:81% position:19%
RIG WORKER: All
right, there's Yancy.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.132 align:start size:97% position:3%
CHRIS: It's a little different
than being home because you
know it is dangerous.

00:03:53.232 --> 00:03:56.335 align:start size:78% position:22%
You never know
when the cables
are gonna break or,

00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:58.037 align:start size:78% position:22%
or the equipment's
gonna fail.

00:03:59.805 --> 00:04:05.611
RIG WORKER: This is our subsea
control panel, this is used to
shut down any blowouts that come

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:07.446
from the subsea floor.

00:04:08.547 --> 00:04:11.985 align:start size:97% position:3%
CHRIS: The guys you work
with a lot of the time they do
have your life in their hands.

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.321
-Busted!

00:04:15.621 --> 00:04:16.522
-We're workin'!

00:04:16.622 --> 00:04:18.291
-Yeah.

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:30.303 align:start size:88% position:13%
ALWIN: That night was
an amazingly calm night.

00:04:33.239 --> 00:04:36.842 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
Very little wind, I
mean it was just ideal.

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:40.713 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
I just noted, uh, mentally
that evening at sunset
how, how pretty it was.

00:04:57.396 --> 00:04:59.198
I was tending to paperwork.

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:03.369 align:start size:91% position:9%
My mate, he noticed quite a
bit of mud falling through
the center of the rig.

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:07.440
I stood up to see what,
see what he was talking
about and I turned around

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.976 align:start size:78% position:22%
and that's when
I noticed the, uh,
mud raining down.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.447 align:start size:88% position:13%
So, I looked up
out the side window,
port side of the vessel,

00:05:16.248 --> 00:05:17.616
and I seen the mud come out.

00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:20.753
When the mud comes out the top
of the derrick, it's not good.

00:05:22.187 --> 00:05:28.260 line:0%
RANDY: My room phone
rang and the person at
the other end of the,

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:32.632 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
uh, line there opened up by
saying, "We have a situation."

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.736 line:0%
He said, "We have mud
going to the crown."

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:41.908 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
I was just horrified.
I said, "Well, do
y'all have it shut in?"

00:05:43.508 --> 00:05:46.045
He said, "Jason is
shutting it in now."

00:05:50.817 --> 00:05:53.786
And he said, "Randy,
we need your help," and
I'll never forget that.

00:05:54.353 --> 00:05:56.022
And I said, "I'll
be right there."

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:13.538 line:0%
ALWIN: I contacted the
Horizon bridge on the VHF
radio and advised them

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:17.510 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the mud coming
down and asked them
what the situation was.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.612
With tension in his voice,
he immediately advised me

00:06:20.712 --> 00:06:22.715 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
they was having
trouble with the well.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.119 line:0%
ANDREA: I came
up to the bridge.

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:29.722 line:0%
At that time I felt a jolt.

00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:43.302 align:start size:84% position:16%
Yancy came over and
turned the CCTV over
to the starboard side,

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:47.507 align:start size:88% position:13%
and that's when I
witnessed mud coming
from the starboard side.

00:06:53.378 --> 00:06:57.916 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
CHRIS: When I woke up, I
heard, like, a loud buzzing,
Uh, I wasn't sure what it was

00:06:58.016 --> 00:07:00.753
and I just thought somebody
was cleaning or something
like that outside my room,

00:07:00.853 --> 00:07:02.455
so I just fell back asleep.

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:10.663 line:0%
MIKE: I heard a hissing
noise, and, and a thump.

00:07:12.865 --> 00:07:15.768
Within seconds of that
I start hearing beeping.

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:18.471 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I'm hearing the
beep, beep, beep, beep,
beep, beep, beep, beep,

00:07:18.571 --> 00:07:20.105
it's, it's continuous.

00:07:20.205 --> 00:07:22.575 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I'm thinking to myself,
"Okay, what, what's going on?"

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:26.145 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm trying to put
all this together in my head
as to the thump, the hissing,

00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:27.480
and now the beeps.

00:07:29.281 --> 00:07:34.486
I hear the engines start
to rev, normal operating
RPMs to way above what

00:07:34.587 --> 00:07:36.055
I'd ever heard
it run before.

00:07:37.255 --> 00:07:39.625 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's continuously,
steadily rising.

00:07:40.492 --> 00:07:43.196
And I knew then that we
were having a problem.

00:07:46.499 --> 00:07:50.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
As I start to push back from
my desk the computer monitor
exploded in front of me.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:53.638 align:start size:78% position:22%
All the lights
in my shop popped.

00:07:53.739 --> 00:07:55.908 align:start size:91% position:9%
The light bulbs themselves
physically popped.

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:58.277
Now I know we're in trouble.

00:07:58.377 --> 00:08:04.250 align:start position:0%
I reached down to grab my door,
and at the, simultaneously of
grabbing the handle,

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:11.490 align:start size:94% position:6%
the engine goes to
a level that is higher
than I can even describe it.

00:08:11.691 --> 00:08:13.593
It's spinning so fast that...

00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:18.964
it just, it stopped spinning,

00:08:19.364 --> 00:08:21.567
(explosion)

00:08:21.867 --> 00:08:26.371
and there was a huge explosion.

00:08:26.472 --> 00:08:31.344
That explosion blew the fire
door that was between me and
those spaces off the hinges.

00:08:33.345 --> 00:08:38.284 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
RANDY: It blew me
probably 20 feet against a
bulkhead, against a wall.

00:08:38.885 --> 00:08:41.354 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I remember then
that the lights went out.

00:08:42.555 --> 00:08:46.726 align:start size:94% position:6%
Power went out, I could
hear everything deathly calm.

00:08:49.628 --> 00:08:51.631 line:0%
CHRIS: Then I started
trying to find my
clothes in the dark.

00:08:52.731 --> 00:08:54.333 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
I grabbed my
boots, put them on.

00:08:56.135 --> 00:08:59.205 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
MIKE: I couldn't see
anything, I couldn't
breathe, there was no oxygen.

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:04.310 align:start size:88% position:13%
I crawled across
the floor, found the
opening, made my way out.

00:09:06.178 --> 00:09:10.082
Had a small pen flashlight
in my pocket that I put in
my mouth to try to, to see,

00:09:10.182 --> 00:09:11.950 align:start size:81% position:19%
I still couldn't see,
I, I didn't know why
I couldn't see,

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:13.452
I just couldn't see anything.

00:09:15.688 --> 00:09:17.756
I made it to the
next door by feel.

00:09:18.757 --> 00:09:21.861 align:start size:91% position:9%
And as I reached the
next door I reached up and
grabbed the handle for it,

00:09:21.961 --> 00:09:23.629
it then exploded.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:38.477 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
RANDY: My next
recollection was...

00:09:40.312 --> 00:09:43.015
that I had a lot of
debris on top of me.

00:09:43.849 --> 00:09:46.552 align:start size:81% position:19%
I tried two different
times to get up.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:50.923
But whatever it was, it
was a substantial weight.

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:56.596 align:start size:91% position:9%
And I told myself, "Either
you get up, or you're
gonna lay here and die."

00:11:06.398 --> 00:11:12.071 line:0%
MIKE: Crawling through the
ECR, my arm wouldn't work,
my left leg wouldn't work,

00:11:12.504 --> 00:11:14.773 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
I couldn't breathe,
I couldn't see.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:18.310 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
I knew I had to get
outside to some fresh air.

00:11:21.313 --> 00:11:23.849
I crawl across the bodies
of, of at least two men.

00:11:24.716 --> 00:11:26.252
I don't know their condition.

00:11:26.352 --> 00:11:28.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm trying to get them
to respond, they're not
responding, I assume they're

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:32.757 align:start size:91% position:9%
dead and keep moving
because I know that I'm in
no condition to help them.

00:11:33.458 --> 00:11:35.194 align:start size:81% position:19%
I can barely help
myself at this point.

00:11:44.069 --> 00:11:45.737 align:start size:78% position:22%
So, I was tripping
and falling,

00:11:46.271 --> 00:11:50.142 align:start size:94% position:6%
trying to make my way to
the outside watertight door.

00:11:52.377 --> 00:11:56.515
I get about halfway across it
and I can actually start to
see light, a dim light so I,

00:11:56.615 --> 00:12:00.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm, assume I'm headed
in the right direction and I
keep going towards the light.

00:12:02.454 --> 00:12:04.223
Eventually I make
my way outside.

00:12:04.824 --> 00:12:07.626 align:start size:88% position:13%
I've got my bearings,
got my eyes cleaned out.

00:12:10.995 --> 00:12:15.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was no walkway,
there were no handrails, and
there was no stairwell left.

00:12:16.001 --> 00:12:19.471
The wall, the handrail,
the walkway, all those
things were missing.

00:12:20.072 --> 00:12:22.608
They were completely blown
off the back of the rig.

00:12:24.276 --> 00:12:25.978 align:start size:91% position:9%
One more step and I
would've went in the water.

00:12:31.049 --> 00:12:34.653 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
RANDY: So I open my
cabin door, right leg
was hung on something,

00:12:34.753 --> 00:12:40.526 line:0%
I don't know what still,
but I pulled it as hard as
I could and it came free.

00:12:42.194 --> 00:12:43.729 line:0%
I attempted to stand up.

00:12:43.829 --> 00:12:47.133
That was the wrong thing
to do because I immediately
stuck my head into smoke.

00:12:48.767 --> 00:12:51.771 align:start size:91% position:9%
And with the, uh, training
that we've all had on the
rig I knew to stay low.

00:12:53.138 --> 00:12:57.376
I got on my hands and knees,
so I was totally disoriented.

00:12:57.776 --> 00:13:01.914 align:start size:91% position:9%
I'd lost orientation on
which way the doorway was.

00:13:03.648 --> 00:13:07.219 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I remember just
sitting there just trying
to think which way is it.

00:13:08.554 --> 00:13:13.825 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then I felt something
that felt like air, and
I said to myself,

00:13:14.459 --> 00:13:16.896 align:start size:91% position:9%
that's the direction I
need to go, that leads out.

00:13:18.831 --> 00:13:24.436 align:start position:0%
So, I had to crawl very
slowly because that end of the
living quarters was pretty well

00:13:24.536 --> 00:13:29.375
demolished, debris everywhere,
but I made it to the doorway,

00:13:31.076 --> 00:13:35.181
and what I thought was
air was actually methane.

00:13:38.283 --> 00:13:42.354
And I could actually feel
like droplets, it was moist
on the side of my face.

00:13:44.757 --> 00:13:47.492
I continued to crawl
down the hallway slowly

00:13:48.627 --> 00:13:53.065
and I put my hand on a body
and it was Wyman Wheeler.

00:14:03.375 --> 00:14:06.412 line:0%
MIKE: There's two lifeboats
there on that lifeboat deck.

00:14:06.979 --> 00:14:13.185 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
And from what I saw, what
I heard, and what I felt, I
seriously considered launching

00:14:13.285 --> 00:14:18.123 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
a lifeboat by myself because
I knew that something really,
really bad had happened

00:14:18.223 --> 00:14:20.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that it wasn't gonna
get any better any time soon.

00:14:21.493 --> 00:14:26.498 align:start size:88% position:13%
I had an inclination
that this was way worse
than anyone could expect.

00:14:28.333 --> 00:14:31.470 align:start size:91% position:9%
And I thought about it for
a second and I remembered
that I had, you know,

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:33.005
I have responsibilities.

00:14:34.472 --> 00:14:37.542 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yeah, I have a emergency
station to go to.

00:14:38.677 --> 00:14:41.347
The problem was my emergency
station no longer existed.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:50.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I made a decision
to put my lifejacket on right
there, and try to make my way

00:14:51.056 --> 00:14:54.994 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the bridge,
which would be my
secondary muster station.

00:14:57.496 --> 00:14:59.164 align:start size:84% position:16%
I determined if I
couldn't make it there,
I was gonna come back

00:14:59.264 --> 00:15:01.333 align:start size:78% position:22%
and launch the
lifeboat by myself.

00:15:08.207 --> 00:15:11.143 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
CHRIS: I'm on the fire
team, so I was trying to
get to my, my fire station.

00:15:12.177 --> 00:15:14.413 line:0%
My main fire station is the
one in the back of the rig.

00:15:16.014 --> 00:15:18.617 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I could see
it and I could see
that nobody was there,

00:15:18.717 --> 00:15:20.386
and there was a bunch
of smoke around it.

00:15:20.486 --> 00:15:22.621
I turned around to go back
to the front of the rig.

00:15:26.624 --> 00:15:28.426 align:start size:91% position:9%
And that's when I saw
the flames on the derrick.

00:15:29.728 --> 00:15:31.830
And soon as I saw that
I knew there was no way
we were gonna put it out.

00:15:32.064 --> 00:15:34.633
We were just gonna get
off of there, I was sure.

00:15:35.601 --> 00:15:37.435
That's when my
panic set in.

00:15:38.503 --> 00:15:41.807 align:start position:0%
I mean, that scared me
absolutely to death, right then.

00:15:42.541 --> 00:15:45.109
I've never had a
feeling like that.

00:15:46.211 --> 00:15:50.949
I mean, the lifeboats were
probably gone, and I didn't
know, I hadn't seen anybody

00:15:51.049 --> 00:15:53.986
until this point, I didn't
know if I was the only person
still on the rig or what.

00:15:57.422 --> 00:16:00.793 align:start size:97% position:3%
Uh, that's all I kept thinking
was, well, we're all dead.

00:16:16.541 --> 00:16:18.210 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
DUSTIN: I was
relieved for the night.

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:20.011 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
On the way home, I
received a phone call.

00:16:20.111 --> 00:16:24.048 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
They said, "We just
had an oil rig explosion,
you need to come back here

00:16:24.148 --> 00:16:26.317
as soon as possible,
five minutes ago."

00:16:27.252 --> 00:16:29.555 align:start size:91% position:9%
So I said, "Okay. I'm
turning around right now."

00:16:31.657 --> 00:16:35.026 align:start size:84% position:16%
I said, "Just
throw my, all my
gear in the helicopter

00:16:35.126 --> 00:16:36.962
and I'll change en route."

00:16:40.332 --> 00:16:43.402 align:start size:84% position:16%
Just ran right to the
helicopter, hopped in,
and off we went.

00:16:46.438 --> 00:16:50.075 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
JOHN: As soon as we took
off I, I put my night vision
goggles down and I could see

00:16:50.175 --> 00:16:52.544 line:0%
this immense glow
on the horizon.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:01.186 align:start size:88% position:13%
It looked like I was
flying to New York City.

00:17:01.286 --> 00:17:05.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
The glow was that
immense and normally you,
you might see a faint horizon

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:07.058
or you see no horizon at all.

00:17:07.258 --> 00:17:10.329
And, uh, I, I told my crew,
I said, "You guys, you see
that light on the horizon?

00:17:11.663 --> 00:17:13.231
That's where we're going to."

00:17:14.499 --> 00:17:18.103
It sunk in right there
that this is, this is real,
this is the real thing.

00:17:19.371 --> 00:17:22.007 align:start size:91% position:9%
We don't know what
we're gonna see when we get
there but we know it's big.

00:17:37.055 --> 00:17:42.360 line:0%
RANDY: The next thing
I recollect is I saw,
like, a beam of light,

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:47.433
like a flashlight bouncing, and
I saw that to be Stan Carden.

00:17:51.136 --> 00:17:55.074 align:start size:81% position:19%
And about that time,
Jimmy Harrel came out
of his room.

00:17:58.309 --> 00:18:01.880 line:0%
He told me he was in
the shower when the
explosion happened.

00:18:03.281 --> 00:18:07.619
He had managed to find a pair
of coveralls and put those on.

00:18:09.588 --> 00:18:11.924 line:0%
And I said, "Jimmy, I've
got Wyman down right here."

00:18:13.157 --> 00:18:17.529 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, we asked him to go to
the bow and get a stretcher.

00:18:19.731 --> 00:18:22.200
We continued to remove
this debris off of Wyman.

00:18:23.335 --> 00:18:28.273
I helped him up and
I was gonna try to
help walk him out,

00:18:28.840 --> 00:18:30.409
thinking that that
might be quicker.

00:18:34.012 --> 00:18:38.216 align:start size:94% position:6%
He made a couple of
steps with his arm around my
shoulder and he was in pain,

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:41.987
and then he said, "Set
me down, set me down."

00:18:43.555 --> 00:18:47.659
So we set him back
down and he said,

00:18:48.994 --> 00:18:54.199 align:start position:0%
"Y'all go on and save yourself."
You know, and I said, "No,
we're not gonna leave you,

00:18:54.732 --> 00:18:56.535 align:start size:78% position:22%
we're not gonna
leave you in here."

00:19:00.706 --> 00:19:03.108 align:start size:81% position:19%
MIKE: Once I got
on the bridge, I
reported immediately

00:19:03.208 --> 00:19:08.613 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
to the captain that we
have no propulsion, we
have no power, and I said,

00:19:08.713 --> 00:19:12.417
"You need to understand,
engine number three,
for sure, has blown up.

00:19:13.451 --> 00:19:15.320
We need to abandon ship now."

00:19:17.488 --> 00:19:19.290 line:0%
STEVE: I turned around, I
didn't recognize who it was

00:19:19.391 --> 00:19:21.727 line:0%
at the time because he
was covered in blood.

00:19:22.561 --> 00:19:24.263 align:start size:81% position:19%
It was Mike Williams,
Chief ET.

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:26.998 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I asked him,
"What do you mean gone?"

00:19:27.098 --> 00:19:28.233
He said, "They've blown up.

00:19:28.333 --> 00:19:30.234
They're all gone,
they've blown up."

00:19:30.735 --> 00:19:32.704
Upon looking at the screen
there was still nothing.

00:19:33.404 --> 00:19:35.740 align:start size:91% position:9%
No engines starting, no
thrusters running, nothing.

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:37.276
We were still a dead ship.

00:19:39.177 --> 00:19:43.581 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
Chris Pleasant, the
subsea supervisor was
standing at the BOP panel.

00:19:44.015 --> 00:19:46.584 align:start size:78% position:22%
I hollered out
to Chris Pleasant,
"Have you EDS'd?"

00:19:46.918 --> 00:19:48.920 line:0%
He said he needed
permission to EDS.

00:19:49.487 --> 00:19:53.825 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
Somebody on the bridge
hollered out he cannot EDS
without the OIM's approval.

00:19:54.626 --> 00:19:56.428 line:0%
I hollered out, "Can we EDS?"

00:19:56.528 --> 00:20:01.733
He said, "Yes, EDS, EDS." When
I turned back to Chris, he was
in the panel pushing a button.

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:13.177 line:0%
PLEASANT: You know, I hit the
EDS and I seen it go to close
and I was looking through that

00:20:13.277 --> 00:20:16.013
panel trying to see
what was going on.

00:20:16.814 --> 00:20:22.153
After I saw that I had no
hydraulics, I had no pressure
in the system that allowed

00:20:22.253 --> 00:20:27.392
those functions to work, I
knew it was time to leave.

00:20:32.263 --> 00:20:35.467 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
STEVE: I hollered to Chris,
"I need confirmation that
we have EDS'd."

00:20:35.734 --> 00:20:38.770
He said yes and he pointed
at a light in the panel.

00:20:40.238 --> 00:20:43.041 align:start position:0% line:0%
PLEASANT: Steve Bertone,
then he turns away and he said,

00:20:43.141 --> 00:20:46.311 line:0%
"I'm going to the emergency
generator room," like that.

00:20:47.079 --> 00:20:51.049 align:start size:91% position:9%
And I said, "You
need me to go with you."
And he didn't say nothing,

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:53.552
but I looked out that door
and I said, "I ain't going."

00:20:58.022 --> 00:21:01.225 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
STEVE: When I left the
bridge, I went to close
the watertight door

00:21:01.325 --> 00:21:04.629
and Mike Williams
pushed the door back
and he, he said,

00:21:04.729 --> 00:21:09.701 align:start position:0%
"You're not going alone, chief."
I said, "Well, come on." Paul
Meinhardt, the motorman,

00:21:09.901 --> 00:21:13.372 align:start size:84% position:16%
also fell in line
and we ran towards the,
uh, standby generator.

00:21:15.373 --> 00:21:18.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
As I was running I looked up
at the derrick, and I could
see nothing but flames,

00:21:19.744 --> 00:21:21.580
because I could see no
equipment, whatsoever.

00:21:23.447 --> 00:21:25.017
It was solid flames.

00:21:27.585 --> 00:21:33.124
When we walked into the, uh,
standby generator room, my
thinking at that point was,

00:21:33.457 --> 00:21:35.960
what remaining fuel would be
in the riser would burn away,

00:21:36.327 --> 00:21:38.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we were gonna need
power as well as fire pumps.

00:21:40.131 --> 00:21:41.799
I hit the start button.

00:21:42.366 --> 00:21:44.569 align:start size:91% position:9%
There was absolutely no
turning over of the engine.

00:21:45.236 --> 00:21:47.973
At that point I said,
"That's it, let's go
back to the bridge.

00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:50.108
Uh, it's not gonna crank."

00:21:54.278 --> 00:21:56.548 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
MIKE: On our way back to
the bridge is when I noticed,

00:21:56.648 --> 00:22:00.885 line:0%
I believe it was lifeboat
number one, had descended,
and was motoring away.

00:22:01.853 --> 00:22:03.655 align:start size:91% position:9%
They had descended and
disconnected from the rig.

00:22:28.613 --> 00:22:31.116 line:0%
CHRIS: We went down to
the life capsule deck.

00:22:31.683 --> 00:22:33.084 line:0%
Everybody was panicking.

00:22:33.184 --> 00:22:36.621 align:start size:91% position:9%
Some people were
trying to stay calm and
do what needed to be done,

00:22:37.055 --> 00:22:38.557
like the Chief Mate, Captain.

00:22:38.757 --> 00:22:40.725 align:start size:91% position:9%
So, they were trying to get
the stuff handled the
way they were trained

00:22:40.825 --> 00:22:42.594
and knew how to handle it.

00:22:44.462 --> 00:22:45.630 align:start size:75% position:25%
Some people
were jumping off,

00:22:50.868 --> 00:22:53.371
some people were holding
people back from jumping
off, they wouldn't let 'em.

00:22:54.339 --> 00:22:56.374
And then other people
were screaming we've
got to get out of here,

00:22:56.474 --> 00:22:57.875
we've got to get out of here.

00:22:58.376 --> 00:23:03.782
So I mean, there was a
certain level of panic
that everybody was in

00:23:04.849 --> 00:23:06.985 align:start size:84% position:16%
that made things a
little more difficult
in getting off the rig.

00:23:08.987 --> 00:23:12.891 align:start size:97% position:3%
I got in the number two
lifeboat, everybody was in
there hollering and screaming,

00:23:13.125 --> 00:23:15.693 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, "We gotta
go. The derrick is
fixin' to fall on us."

00:23:16.661 --> 00:23:19.831
They finally got
everybody in there
and shut the door.

00:23:22.467 --> 00:23:25.303
Then they released
the lifeboat to,
down to the water.

00:23:29.741 --> 00:23:32.944 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
MIKE: As we were making
our way down the ladder
way to get to the lifeboats,

00:23:33.044 --> 00:23:34.246
lifeboat number two descended.

00:23:37.949 --> 00:23:40.585 align:start size:88% position:13%
So now there
are, the two forward
lifeboats are both gone,

00:23:40.785 --> 00:23:42.153
they're both unavailable.

00:23:42.254 --> 00:23:45.257 align:start size:88% position:13%
Once they go down
there's no coming back up
because we had no power.

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:52.964 align:start size:84% position:16%
There was several minor
explosions occurring,
things are falling,

00:23:53.064 --> 00:23:54.466
you can hear stuff popping.

00:24:10.514 --> 00:24:15.220 line:0%
RANDY: It seemed like
an eternity, they came
back with a stretcher.

00:24:17.155 --> 00:24:19.191 align:start size:88% position:13%
We were able to get
Wyman on that stretcher.

00:24:21.192 --> 00:24:24.296 align:start size:84% position:16%
When we got outside
of the living quarters

00:24:25.296 --> 00:24:27.064
the first thing
I observed was

00:24:28.199 --> 00:24:31.936 align:start size:91% position:9%
both of the main lifeboats
had already been deployed
and had left.

00:24:33.137 --> 00:24:39.477 align:start size:88% position:13%
I also looked to my left
and I saw Captain Curt
and a few of his crew

00:24:40.211 --> 00:24:43.514
starting to deploy a life raft.

00:24:43.781 --> 00:24:46.151
And we continued
down the walkway

00:24:46.251 --> 00:24:50.621
until we got to that life
raft and we were able to

00:24:51.656 --> 00:24:57.395
catch the head part of the
stretcher and assist getting
Wyman into the life raft,

00:24:59.263 --> 00:25:04.102 align:start size:91% position:9%
and I think we actually
fell, trying to, you know,
get him into the life raft.

00:25:04.436 --> 00:25:07.505
But the main thing
is Wyman was there.

00:25:08.105 --> 00:25:09.875
You know, he didn't
get left behind.

00:25:16.548 --> 00:25:22.587 line:0%
STEVE: There was a lot
of explosions still going
on and immense heat.

00:25:23.722 --> 00:25:29.194
All the flames and heat from
the rig floor were coming down
the forward part of that deck

00:25:29.527 --> 00:25:33.264
as well as all the flames and
the heat from under the rig
that were meeting I guess in

00:25:33.364 --> 00:25:35.767
like a vortex or something
right there at the life raft.

00:25:36.501 --> 00:25:41.072 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I can remember feeling the
intense heat on my knees, and
I also heard screaming

00:25:41.539 --> 00:25:43.241
"We're gonna die,
we're gonna die."

00:25:45.176 --> 00:25:47.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I honestly
thought that we were
gonna cook right there.

00:25:54.319 --> 00:25:56.554 line:0%
MIKE: I wasn't sure that the
life raft was gonna survive.

00:25:56.954 --> 00:26:00.358 align:start size:88% position:13%
There was a crowd of
folks there trying to get
into this small opening.

00:26:03.628 --> 00:26:07.999
There was so much heat coming
up I thought for sure the life
raft was gonna pop or melt

00:26:08.433 --> 00:26:09.935 align:start size:81% position:19%
and the people inside
were gonna cook.

00:26:22.747 --> 00:26:25.784 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> REPORTER (over TV): Moments
 after the rig first exploded
 you can hear the roar.</i>

00:26:27.318 --> 00:26:30.621
<i> It's the sound of escaping
 gas, feeding the fireball,</i>

00:26:30.721 --> 00:26:32.357
<i> incinerating the
 Deepwater Horizon.</i>

00:26:41.232 --> 00:26:43.635 line:0%
ALWIN: We was
approximately
100 meters out.

00:26:53.545 --> 00:26:57.047 align:start position:0% line:0%
I gave the command to
launch shortly after we've seen

00:26:57.147 --> 00:26:59.985 align:start size:81% position:19%
the first three guys
jump off the rig.

00:27:20.338 --> 00:27:25.443 line:0%
AL: I knew this was big,
I've never seen flames that
gigantic before, that huge.

00:27:27.245 --> 00:27:29.381 align:start size:81% position:19%
This was like seeing
hell on earth.

00:27:39.357 --> 00:27:42.026 line:0%
STEVE: The next thing I knew
the life raft was descending.

00:27:42.126 --> 00:27:45.764 line:0%
When we touched the water
I heard somebody holler
out, where are the paddles?

00:27:47.165 --> 00:27:52.837
I jumped out of the life
raft, Chad Murray jumped
out, was right behind me,

00:27:54.338 --> 00:27:58.042 align:start position:0%
and grabbed hold of the
rope on the side of that
life raft and started swimming,

00:27:58.142 --> 00:27:59.844 align:start size:78% position:22%
trying to pull
the life raft away.

00:28:01.245 --> 00:28:03.681 align:start size:91% position:9%
I was swimming on my side,
looking up at the rig.

00:28:04.748 --> 00:28:10.955 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would say 25, 30 feet above
me there was a tremendous
amount of smoke billowing out

00:28:11.055 --> 00:28:12.390
from under the rig.

00:28:23.201 --> 00:28:25.503 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
YANCY: I asked the
captain what about us.

00:28:25.603 --> 00:28:28.473 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
And he said I don't know about
you, but I'm going to jump.

00:28:30.742 --> 00:28:32.210
So he actually jumped first.

00:28:34.578 --> 00:28:37.114 align:start size:91% position:9%
I waited for a minute
because when I looked over

00:28:37.214 --> 00:28:40.151 align:start size:91% position:9%
the life raft was basically
right underneath me.

00:28:42.754 --> 00:28:44.956
So I waited for a minute

00:28:45.556 --> 00:28:48.993 align:start size:81% position:19%
so it could actually
move out the way

00:28:51.862 --> 00:28:53.698
and, uh, I jumped.

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:09.848
When I resurfaced
I had the life raft

00:29:11.516 --> 00:29:13.284
was like 10 or 15
feet away from me.

00:29:15.319 --> 00:29:18.156 align:start size:81% position:19%
So I swam to the
life raft, and I seen

00:29:20.324 --> 00:29:23.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
the captain and a few
other guys that were on
the outside of the life raft.

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:33.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
STEVE: As we're swimming
trying to pull this life raft
away from the rig I got to a

00:29:33.203 --> 00:29:35.606
point where I could
see the helideck.

00:29:37.075 --> 00:29:41.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
I witnessed an individual
running at full speed across
the helideck, when he jumped

00:29:42.046 --> 00:29:44.482 align:start size:91% position:9%
off the end of the helideck
he was still running.

00:29:45.149 --> 00:29:48.953 align:start size:94% position:6%
Just before he splashed
into the water he was
actually looking over at us.

00:29:57.161 --> 00:30:01.132 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
MIKE: In our training,
they teach you to reach your
hand around your life jacket,

00:30:01.232 --> 00:30:05.102 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
take one step off,
look straight ahead,
cross your legs, and fall.

00:30:05.736 --> 00:30:08.373
The problem with that is
there's now a life raft
down there at the bottom.

00:30:10.141 --> 00:30:11.643
Maybe 90 feet, 100 feet.

00:30:14.112 --> 00:30:15.947
It's a long ways.

00:30:17.081 --> 00:30:19.284 align:start size:81% position:19%
So I took off
running and I jumped.

00:30:24.388 --> 00:30:27.292
I cleared the life raft
by a pretty good ways.

00:30:30.561 --> 00:30:36.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
Once I hit the water,
when I came back up I
couldn't see anything again,

00:30:41.005 --> 00:30:42.740
because now I've got
a new set of problems.

00:30:43.540 --> 00:30:47.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
Oil, hydraulic fluid,
gasoline, diesel, whatever
it is that's floating on the

00:30:47.144 --> 00:30:50.849 align:start size:81% position:19%
water is now burning
my entire body.

00:30:52.082 --> 00:30:54.185
I'm now covered
in this sludge.

00:30:54.285 --> 00:30:57.321 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't know what it is,
it's burning I can't
hardly breathe,

00:30:58.289 --> 00:31:00.959 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I could feel
the heat from the fire
underneath the vessel.

00:31:02.726 --> 00:31:06.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
At that point I started back
stroking with the one arm
and one leg that would work.

00:31:07.764 --> 00:31:11.135 align:start size:88% position:13%
Until I remember
feeling no pain, I
remember feeling no heat,

00:31:12.036 --> 00:31:16.774 align:start size:88% position:13%
and thinking that
that was it, I had died.

00:31:22.413 --> 00:31:26.251
Sometime later, something
apparently woke me back up,

00:31:27.852 --> 00:31:29.620 align:start size:81% position:19%
a pop, or explosion,
something.

00:31:30.087 --> 00:31:33.057 align:start size:91% position:9%
And I remember feeling
the heat again, starting
to feel the pain come back,

00:31:33.791 --> 00:31:36.794 align:start size:88% position:13%
thinking I've gotta swim,
I've gotta swim and I
started swimming again.

00:31:41.132 --> 00:31:44.102
Then I heard something
in the distance, I heard
"Over here, over here."

00:31:45.603 --> 00:31:47.805 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was thinking to myself what
in the world can that be?

00:31:49.006 --> 00:31:51.408
Whatever it is I'm gonna go to
it and just started swimming

00:31:51.508 --> 00:31:53.244 align:start size:78% position:22%
as hard as I could
to get to it.

00:31:56.447 --> 00:31:58.916 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then I felt
something start lifting
me out of the water.

00:32:00.351 --> 00:32:06.424 align:start size:91% position:9%
A small orange rescue craft
had grabbed me, and flipped
me over into the boat.

00:32:10.561 --> 00:32:12.630
At that point the guy said
there's a raft in the water.

00:32:15.399 --> 00:32:17.168
We proceeded to go
towards the rig.

00:32:19.303 --> 00:32:22.040 align:start position:0%
Now we're close enough that I
can feel the heat, I'm starting
to feel the heat again,

00:32:24.175 --> 00:32:28.746 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I see the life
raft and it's literally
still under the rig.

00:32:30.181 --> 00:32:31.816 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I can see
people outside of it.

00:32:32.283 --> 00:32:35.420
We get up to 'em, throw 'em
a line, they get tied on.

00:32:37.154 --> 00:32:39.757 line:0%
YANCY: Then the fast
rescue boat pulled us
to the starboard side

00:32:39.857 --> 00:32:41.326 line:0%
of the Bankston.

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:47.398 align:start size:88% position:13%
The Bankston crew was
throwing down Jacobs
ladders to the lifeboats,

00:32:47.498 --> 00:32:49.834 align:start size:84% position:16%
to where people inside
can get up on deck.

00:33:00.878 --> 00:33:03.982 align:start size:97% position:3%
I seen Carl, the rig operator,
he was taking muster.

00:33:07.018 --> 00:33:09.320
I asked him how many
people we were missing.

00:33:10.388 --> 00:33:12.523
Come to find out it was 11.

00:33:33.144 --> 00:33:38.182 line:0%
JOHN: We're flying as close
as a half mile, to a quarter
of a mile, circling the rig,

00:33:38.282 --> 00:33:40.150 align:start size:84% position:16%
looking for
survivors in the water.

00:33:40.884 --> 00:33:43.320
We smelled the smoke, we
smelled the oil, the fumes.

00:33:44.588 --> 00:33:48.359 align:start size:91% position:9%
We felt the heat
through the cockpit, we
felt it in the helicopters,

00:33:48.459 --> 00:33:49.727
you know, through
the windshields.

00:33:51.362 --> 00:33:54.932 align:start size:91% position:9%
It really hits home and
strikes you that hey, we're
flying at 300 feet and the

00:33:55.032 --> 00:33:58.803 align:start size:94% position:6%
flames are higher than we
are, probably up to 500 feet.

00:34:02.205 --> 00:34:07.511 line:0%
AL: I was looking into
this burning hulk of metal
and it was like looking

00:34:07.611 --> 00:34:08.980
into the face of the devil.

00:34:11.215 --> 00:34:14.052 align:start size:81% position:19%
It was, it was
like a living thing.

00:34:21.058 --> 00:34:25.329 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
YANCY: They already had
boats that were putting
water on the rig.

00:34:27.531 --> 00:34:28.699 align:start size:78% position:22%
I don't know where
they came from.

00:34:28.799 --> 00:34:31.369
They came out the wood work.
They were there in no time.

00:34:36.106 --> 00:34:38.643 align:start size:91% position:9%
The Coast Guard helicopters
were flying around.

00:34:41.312 --> 00:34:45.315 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
AL: We were hovering
about half a mile to the
East of the burning rig.

00:34:45.916 --> 00:34:50.654
Initially we got the word
that we were to pick up a
critically wounded victim

00:34:50.754 --> 00:34:53.758
so then we lowered our rescue
swimmer to the Damon Bankston.

00:34:55.793 --> 00:34:58.996 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
COAST GUARD:
Moving out swimmer,
moving out swimmer.

00:34:59.730 --> 00:35:04.168 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
JOHN: The rescue swimmer,
he's basically our paramedic,
he's our assigned EMT,

00:35:04.268 --> 00:35:06.603 line:0%
so he's the guy who
knows the first aid.

00:35:07.738 --> 00:35:11.409 align:start size:97% position:3%
COAST GUARD: Roger, hold
position I'mma bring him down.
Swimmer's just below the rail.

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:16.280
Halfway down, 40 right, 40.

00:35:20.584 --> 00:35:21.818
Okay swimmers on deck.

00:35:21.918 --> 00:35:24.955 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
STEVE: When the
Coast Guard arrived,
the rescue swimmer,

00:35:25.056 --> 00:35:27.992 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
he came in and he asked
who's the critical.

00:35:29.693 --> 00:35:31.062
Buddy Trahan was the worst.

00:35:32.096 --> 00:35:33.897
And at that point they
brought in a gurney.

00:35:33.997 --> 00:35:37.667 align:start size:94% position:6%
I stepped to the backside
of the bed to assist in
getting Buddy on the gurney.

00:35:38.668 --> 00:35:42.739 align:start size:94% position:6%
As I rolled him he was
screaming and hollering that
his leg was hurt real bad,

00:35:42.839 --> 00:35:45.709 align:start size:84% position:16%
he had a severe
laceration on his leg.

00:35:46.510 --> 00:35:52.583 align:start size:91% position:9%
He also had a twisted
and mangled lower-calf,
his fingernails were gone,

00:35:53.451 --> 00:35:54.852 align:start size:81% position:19%
he had a hole in
the side of his neck.

00:35:55.853 --> 00:36:02.126
And I looked back and
Buddy's back was burnt
from belt to head.

00:36:04.095 --> 00:36:06.364
Once we got him on the
gurney they took him out.

00:36:11.168 --> 00:36:15.973 line:0%
DUSTIN: We went to carry him
out after we strapped him in
and the supply boat was full

00:36:16.073 --> 00:36:22.413
of big containers, so you
really had no clear path
to the back of the boat.

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:25.616
So we were like, "How are
we gonna get this guy out?"

00:36:26.550 --> 00:36:29.921
We actually had to lift him
up over these containers

00:36:31.955 --> 00:36:33.791
and got him hoisted
up to the helicopter.

00:36:39.496 --> 00:36:43.100 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
AL: It's hard when you're
departing a scene like that,

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:46.503 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
to know that there's
still 11 people missing.

00:36:47.304 --> 00:36:51.375 align:start size:91% position:9%
Our mission was to
return to base with a
critically injured victim,

00:36:51.475 --> 00:36:54.278
but it still felt bad
to leave the scene.

00:36:55.045 --> 00:36:57.114
But that was the
task we were given.

00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:03.186 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
JESSICA: It began with
a powerful explosion, a
column of flames shooting into

00:37:03.286 --> 00:37:05.656 line:0%
the night sky over
the Gulf of Mexico.

00:37:06.757 --> 00:37:10.394
The oil rig still burning
and could topple into
the water at any moment.

00:37:12.429 --> 00:37:16.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
MARY: The source of that
fire is predominantly coming
from the well head itself.

00:37:16.467 --> 00:37:19.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's crude oil that is
leeching from this wellhead.

00:37:19.803 --> 00:37:24.709
And as long as that crude
is leeching we're gonna
continue to see that fire.

00:37:28.379 --> 00:37:31.949 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
YANCY: After the helicopter
left, and it was like,
with the wounded,

00:37:32.049 --> 00:37:34.085 line:0%
it was like everything just
kind of like fell in place.

00:37:35.786 --> 00:37:38.255 align:start size:78% position:22%
Finally able to
start calming down.

00:37:39.890 --> 00:37:45.429 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
CHRIS: And we sat there
pretty much watched the rig
burn for six or seven hours.

00:37:48.799 --> 00:37:50.501
That's something
I'll never forget.

00:37:51.302 --> 00:37:54.738
The images just burned in
my head, I'll never forget.

00:37:55.238 --> 00:37:58.042
Knowing that, after we took the
muster and stuff we figured out,

00:37:58.142 --> 00:37:59.876
you know, we were
missing 11 guys.

00:38:00.877 --> 00:38:05.816 align:start size:91% position:9%
And, most of us, we assumed
they were, they were on the
rig, we didn't know,

00:38:06.150 --> 00:38:08.286
and that was pretty
tough not knowing.

00:38:10.354 --> 00:38:13.557 line:0%
DUSTIN: I went home that
morning and turned on the
news and then you find out

00:38:13.657 --> 00:38:16.626 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
well there's still
11 people missing,
it's like "Oh."

00:38:17.794 --> 00:38:20.398 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's like, "Man, I
hope they made, I just
hope they made it off."

00:38:32.209 --> 00:38:35.446 line:0%
CURRY: In the news, the Coast
Guard is pressing its search
today for 11 workers still

00:38:35.546 --> 00:38:39.150 line:0%
missing on a burning oil
drilling platform off
the coast of Louisiana.

00:38:39.483 --> 00:38:41.752
Fireboats are struggling
to put out flames triggered

00:38:41.852 --> 00:38:43.587
by the explosion
on Tuesday night.

00:38:45.856 --> 00:38:49.392 line:0%
CHRIS: When we left
from the rig, we rode
for probably a good

00:38:49.493 --> 00:38:51.294 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
four hours to
another platform.

00:38:52.629 --> 00:38:56.600
The guys on that platform,
they sent down a bag with some
cigarettes, and some snuff,

00:38:56.833 --> 00:38:58.469
and stuff, and some clothes.

00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:03.206
JEFFREY: The survivors who
were not injured are now
making their way to shore.

00:39:03.306 --> 00:39:05.509 line:0%
They will be arriving here
in port later this evening.

00:39:05.609 --> 00:39:08.345 line:0%
The search for the
missing will continue
through the night.

00:39:09.413 --> 00:39:11.948 align:start size:91% position:9%
RON: A pair of Coast Guard
cutters stayed out in the
gulf all night looking

00:39:12.048 --> 00:39:13.550
for these missing workers.

00:39:13.650 --> 00:39:15.886 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
They will be joined
a little bit later by
one of these choppers,

00:39:15.986 --> 00:39:17.788 line:0%
which is set to leave
at first light here.

00:39:17.888 --> 00:39:19.990 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
And as you can imagine,
the longer this search
and rescue mission

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:24.161 align:start size:84% position:16%
goes without
resolution, the more
fear and concern grow.

00:39:30.534 --> 00:39:34.671 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
CHRIS: When we got off the
boat they brought around three
or four pre-paid cellphones.

00:39:36.339 --> 00:39:40.243 align:start position:0%
They were passing those around,
everybody was passing them,
talking to their families

00:39:40.343 --> 00:39:43.213
for about 30 seconds
just letting them know
they were off the boat.

00:39:43.880 --> 00:39:45.916
That was the first time
I got to talk to my wife.

00:39:50.887 --> 00:39:55.225
From there we rode
to New Orleans to
the Crowne Plaza.

00:39:56.727 --> 00:40:00.130 align:start size:97% position:3%
We went in the back of
it, because they told us there
were so many lawyers and media

00:40:00.230 --> 00:40:04.235
out front, they were making
us go in the back, and all
our families were waiting,

00:40:06.536 --> 00:40:08.505 align:start size:91% position:9%
and that was the first
time I got to see my wife.

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:15.145
And she ran up smiling, and
then by the time she got her
arms around my neck she just

00:40:15.245 --> 00:40:18.115 align:start size:78% position:22%
burst out in tears,
both of us did.

00:40:27.524 --> 00:40:29.593 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
JAMES: We were on
site about midnight,

00:40:29.693 --> 00:40:33.296 align:start position:0% line:0%
so we were only on site in a
relatively short period of time.

00:40:34.230 --> 00:40:39.403 align:start size:91% position:9%
I started to have a bad
feeling when the sun came
up and we could get a more

00:40:39.503 --> 00:40:43.574
accurate assessment of
the extent of the damage
onboard the oil rig.

00:40:45.843 --> 00:40:50.247
You could see the derrick
leaning over on the crane
and you could actually see

00:40:50.347 --> 00:40:54.819 align:start size:91% position:9%
what used to be
incredibly stout steel
sagging like a clothesline.

00:40:56.320 --> 00:41:01.592
It got very precarious
at that time and we were
all very, very concerned

00:41:01.792 --> 00:41:04.395
that time was running out.

00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:11.201 align:start size:97% position:3%
But at around 10:00, we
could see the starboard corner

00:41:11.301 --> 00:41:14.038
starting to slowly
drop into the sea.

00:41:16.206 --> 00:41:20.477 align:start size:88% position:13%
The loud groans
coming out of this
vessel, as she broke up,

00:41:21.011 --> 00:41:22.613
was becoming much
more pronounced.

00:41:23.847 --> 00:41:25.382
We started to back off,

00:41:27.484 --> 00:41:31.288 align:start size:81% position:19%
a thick sheen of
brown emulsified oil
still on fire behind.

00:41:35.625 --> 00:41:39.930 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think unfortunately
for us we were fighting a
losing battle from the start.

00:41:43.400 --> 00:41:45.235
But you still feel
a tremendous loss,

00:41:45.469 --> 00:41:47.371 align:start size:75% position:25%
you still feel a
tremendous sense
of failure.

00:41:53.476 --> 00:41:54.945 align:start size:78% position:22%
My thinking
immediately shifted

00:41:57.447 --> 00:42:00.884 line:0%
to that of the
people onboard.

00:42:02.252 --> 00:42:06.857 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
You're there to help
the loved ones of those
people that may be missing

00:42:07.291 --> 00:42:10.160 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
come to some
sense of closure.

00:42:11.528 --> 00:42:16.866 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
It was quite a silence on
the bridge, and I know we were
all thinking the same thing.

00:42:18.067 --> 00:42:24.374 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
You feel the loss, you feel
the failure, you feel it deep.

00:42:25.675 --> 00:42:32.016 line:0%
And it settles in more and
more as time goes by and
as the adrenaline subsides.

00:42:33.584 --> 00:42:37.254 line:0%
You know, I'm still going
over things in my mind.

00:42:39.823 --> 00:42:42.626 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> REPORTER (over TV): There
 were 126 crewmembers on
 board the rig.</i>

00:42:42.860 --> 00:42:47.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Eleven of the workers
 never had a chance, they
 were killed in the explosion,</i>

00:42:47.231 --> 00:42:49.066
<i> their bodies never recovered.</i>

00:42:51.201 --> 00:42:57.541 line:0%
KEITH: These guys on the
Deepwater are the fighter
pilots of our profession.

00:42:58.208 --> 00:42:59.977
They are the rock stars.

00:43:00.978 --> 00:43:05.515 line:0%
Only the very best of the
best work on the Deepwater.

00:43:06.182 --> 00:43:09.053 align:start size:81% position:19%
And they don't
accept anything less.

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:47.157 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
RANDY: The Horizon
was an exemplary rig
with excellent personnel.

00:43:47.957 --> 00:43:54.030 line:0%
They paid the ultimate price,
they gave their life, to try
to minimize the damage that

00:43:54.130 --> 00:43:57.301 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
was done to people,
property, environment.

00:43:57.634 --> 00:44:03.173 line:0%
And I hope at least that
message is clear, that they
paid the ultimate price.

00:44:09.112 --> 00:44:10.247 line:0%
Captioned by Cotter
Captioning Services.

